<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hor-Aha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 3100 BCE</span> · Also known as Aha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hor-Aha was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, around 3100 BCE, the successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a>, and is often identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menes">Menes</a>, the legendary first king. Labels from his reign record campaigns and religious foundations, and tradition credits him with the founding of Memphis. His tomb at Abydos was surrounded by the graves of sacrificed retainers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hor-aha.jpg" alt="Hor-Aha" width="320" height="354" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GlazedFiaenceVesselFragmentNameOfAha-BritishMuseum-August21-08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
